 "Mochi DoneStamp": 1,
 "When did you last change the sheets or clean the AC filter? The easiest answer is a one-tap log of the moment you finish. How it differs from recurring reminders, and how to start with Mochi DoneStamp.": 1,
 "An app that answers \"when did I last do that?\": log the last date for sheets, filters and watering": 1,
 "Most people searching for a \"when did I last\" app don't want another to-do list. They want to remember the last time they did things that aren't daily but matter when forgotten: changing the sheets, replacing a toothbrush, cleaning an air-conditioner filter.": 1,
 "When Did I Last? Home Upkeep": 1,
 "Log the date and time with one tap the moment you finish. Recording what you actually did and seeing how many days have passed works better than piling up reminders in advance. Add a rhythm only to things with a real interval, and count the next time from the day you actually finished.": 1,
 "You lose track because you record when you remember, not when you finish. Writing it into a calendar later means the date is already fuzzy. Reminders tell you the day something is due, but if you do it late or early, every later date drifts. So the habit that lasts is a single tap that stamps the time the moment the job is done.": 1,
 "It pays off most for things done every two weeks to every six months: changing sheets and pillowcases, cleaning the shower drain, AC or range-hood filters, water-filter cartridges, watering and repotting plants, replacing toothbrushes and sponges, calling family, backing up phone photos, haircuts and dental checkups. Each is a classic \"last month… or the month before?\" item.": 1,
 "Then think about rhythms. Put a deadline on everything and you'll get so many alerts you start ignoring them, so add a loose rhythm such as \"about every two weeks\" or an exact one such as \"every 90 days\" only where it matters. The key is that the next time is counted from the day you actually finished. Change the sheets three days late and the next change is two weeks from then.": 1,
 "Mochi DoneStamp is built for exactly this. One tap logs a finished task, with Undo, and you can add or edit past dates. It supports rhythms from minutes to years, local reminders with Done and Snooze, Home and Lock Screen widgets, Siri and Shortcuts, and optional notes and photos. The free version starts with one active event and unlimited history; a one-time Lifetime Pro purchase removes the event limit. No account, no ads, no tracking and no subscription.": 1,
